  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nSteady light showers have remained mainly near and south of a line \nfrom Watertown to Miller today, with sprinkles/drizzle and fog being \nthe main concern. There are still a few pockets of lower visibility \nthat will continue to improve from north to south this afternoon \ninto this evening. Pockets of 1 mile or less visibility are most \nlikely to remain into this evening across western Corson, Campbell, \nMcPherson and Edmunds Counties. The warm front near our southern \ncounties will continue to push north and reside mainly north of the \nND/SD border by 03Z Sunday as the surface low shifts across far \nwestern SD. This should help improve visibilities and bring about at \nleast some temporary relief to the lower clouds. An additional round \nof moisture will push in in advance of the next low across WY/CO \nSunday evening with showers becoming widespread. \n\nThe more steady moderate rainfall will behind late Sunday evening, \nwith much of the rain falling during the daytime hours Monday. \nRainfall amounts continue to show a large spread, but confidence is \nincreasing as the time nears that rainfall amounts of over half an \ninch will be more and more likely (even across north central SD \nwhere the lightest amounts are anticipated). There is a 25 to 35 \npercent chance of receiving 3 inches or more rain by Tuesday \nafternoon over portions of northeastern South Dakota and west \ncentral Minnesota. The lightest rainfall amounts, totaling generally \nless than an inch are forecast west of a line from Mobridge to Eagle \nButte. We will need to monitor river/stream levels due to this \nprolonged period of rain. The WPC Excessive Rainfall Forecast is in \nthe Marginal category for the entire area. There is also a General \nRisk of thunderstorms over our far southeastern counties for this \ntime period (Monday) so brief heavy pockets of rain will not be \ncompletely out of the question.\n\nOtherwise, winds will be a concern for portions of central SD behind \nthe exiting low. Winds out of the north will gust 45 to 55 mph late \nMonday afternoon into Monday evening near and west of the Missouri \nRiver, and linger over portions of south central South Dakota into \nearly Tuesday afternoon.",
